<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- The contract versions ITSELF, independently of the gg binary.
         Declared by hand and held to account by ContractSurfaceTests: a
         fingerprint of the wire surface is recorded against each version in
         contract-versions.json, and changing the surface without moving this
         number fails the build.

         It used to be 0.1.0-alpha.N where N was the commit count on main, so a
         README fix bumped the wire contract exactly as much as a new message
         type did. A consumer seeing the number move learned nothing.

         Pre-1.0: any wire change is a minor bump, and there is no compatibility
         promise. GgVersions.Protocol is a different number and moves only when
         the protocol itself becomes incompatible. -->
    <Version>0.114.0</Version>

    <!-- AssemblyVersion is NOT pinned any more, and that is the point.

         It used to be, at 0.10.0.0, because both fingerprints recorded
         Type.FullName and a constructed generic renders its argument
         assembly-qualified - so the version leaked into every recorded surface
         and bumping this file's Version made both ledgers report a change that
         had not happened.

         The names are normalised now (SurfaceNaming), so nothing about the
         assembly reaches a fingerprint, and three tests assert that silence
         directly. The pin was a workaround for a defect that no longer
         exists. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>GlyphGuild.Gg.Contracts</PackageId>
    <Description>Wire protocol contracts for Good Grief (gg): console, runner, control plane.</Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- THE ONE PACKAGE REFERENCE THIS PROJECT HAS, and it was a deliberate
       reversal rather than an oversight.

       This project carried ZERO, asserted by ContractsDependencyTests, because
       it is the public artifact a customer audits and should not inherit
       anyone else's framework. EnvelopeContractsTests said the same thing from
       the other side, in a test named "the emitter is here and the parser is
       not": model -> text is deterministic and hand-written, text -> model
       needs a grammar, and every grammar is a package.

       ADR-0018 § 5 makes the control plane parse a repository's narrowing
       ITSELF, because a runner reporting "no narrowing here" is a silent
       weakening and Article IX admits no exception. The control plane
       references this package and nothing else. So the parser has to be
       reachable from here, and the alternatives - a second published asset, or
       a third package holding only the parser - were weighed and declined.

       What is bought: parse and render in ONE assembly, which is what
       ADR-0013's `parse(render(x)) == x` obligation has always wanted. Their
       being in two is how `evidence:` stayed authorable, load-bearing at the
       gate, and emitted by neither path for three contract versions.

       What is paid: a customer auditing this package now sees YamlDotNet. The
       guards are amended rather than deleted - exactly one reference, named,
       and a second one still fails. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="YamlDotNet" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
